    id: 10, category: "ai", channel: "Matthew Berman", initials: "MB", date: "Nov 3, 2026", sourcePublished: "2026-11-03", edition: "2026-11-03", minutes: 7, videoMinutes: 13, views: "235K views", subscribers: "600K subscribers",
    title: "If software is cheap to generate, what remains valuable?",
    excerpt: "Frontier coding agents can now build useful personal tools in hours. That does not make all software worthless, but it shifts value away from generic interfaces and toward data, distribution, reliability, security, and continuously operated systems.",
    lede: "Berman's provocation begins with a PDF reader he did not want to install. Instead of accepting a bloated incumbent product, he had an agent build the tool he wanted—then repeated the exercise with analytics software and an Excel-like application.",
    sections: [
      ["The make-or-buy calculation changed", "Model quality, long-horizon tool use, and token prices improved at the same time. A capable agent can now scaffold an interface, implement features, test the result, and iterate for a cost comparable to a month or two of many subscriptions.", "For a one-person use case, feature completeness matters less than exact fit. The user does not need a market, support organization, or polished onboarding; the tool only has to solve that person's problem."],
      ["Examples make the thesis concrete", "Berman shows a lightweight PDF reader with annotation and question-answering, a YouTube outlier tracker built around his workflow, and a browser spreadsheet clone. He argues that tasks which once justified recurring SaaS fees can increasingly be reproduced or open-sourced.", "Decompilation and cloning raise the competitive pressure further, but visual similarity is not the same as a mature product. Edge cases, file compatibility, permissions, collaboration, compliance, and years of operational learning remain difficult to reproduce."],
      ["Software is not only source code", "The strongest version of the zero-value claim is too broad. Code generation lowers the cost of implementation; it does not eliminate the value of trusted data, network effects, distribution, brand, uptime, integrations, and accountability when something fails.", "Security is also dynamic. Agents can patch and monitor custom software continuously, as Berman suggests, but an individual must still manage dependencies, credentials, backups, and the risk that generated code contains subtle vulnerabilities."],
      ["A market of personal software", "The credible shift is from buying a fixed application to commissioning an evolving one. Open-source projects provide a starting point, agents customize them, and an AI-native operating environment can let users reshape workflows without waiting for a vendor roadmap.", "Incumbents will not vanish; they will compete against the option to build. Generic utilities face the most pressure, while products embedded in institutional workflows may become more valuable precisely because they bundle maintenance, governance, and trust."],
    ],
    takeaways: ["Agentic coding sharply lowers the minimum viable scale for useful software.", "Exact personal fit can matter more than mass-market polish.", "Code is becoming cheaper faster than operations, trust, and distribution.", "The likely future is build-plus-maintain, not a one-time prompt that eliminates vendors."],
    timestamps: [["00:00",0,"The PDF-reader trigger"],["02:02",122,"Model quality and token economics"],["04:02",242,"Cloning a spreadsheet application"],["08:00",480,"Agents, databases, and the SaaS thesis"],["10:00",600,"Where custom software still falls short"]],
